186443229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443229 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.09,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.09)=0.20.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.06)=0.05.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1812d74c-f7ff-5b92-ad0c-d300745fe3f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443229 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.09$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.09}{0.06}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(1.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.20$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.20}{0.05}=4.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.5)^{p+1} & ≈4 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(4) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.419… \\\\ p & ≈2.419….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443230 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d0aee14-de07-5ea6-8cb4-30e573b28550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443230 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443231 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.410.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.026.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34486fa6-cccf-5fd8-a975-d4b80d4ead78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443231 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.410$ to $0.026.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.410}{0.026}=15.769….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈15.769… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.769…) \\\\ p & ≈3.979….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443232 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈1.215.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42270ee1-30c3-599c-b59e-da86dc2e3b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443232 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={3}^{5}=243.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.215}{243}=0.005.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443233 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+9(Δx)^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99c7bcc8-5821-518d-9fe0-5a2eb829c883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443233 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+9(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4879a58-4027-569c-b7a3-4f905f12c0fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443234 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =4(0.6)^{3}+(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.216+0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =0.993\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.994.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.993\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.993\\,6}{0.033\\,6}=29.571\\,42…≈29.571.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈29.571\\,42… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(29.571\\,42…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.083… \\\\ p & ≈2.083….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1071 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.4$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a67eecd4-7182-53b5-9971-e13bae152b30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443235 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =5(0.4)^{4}+(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0256+1\\cdot 0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.128+0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.138\\,24 \\\\  & ≈0.138.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}+(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0016+1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008+0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008\\,32 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.138\\,24$ to $0.008\\,32.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.138\\,24}{0.008\\,32}=16.615\\,384…≈16.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈16.615\\,384… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(16.615\\,384…) \\\\ p & ≈4.054….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443236 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443236 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443236 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443236 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.10)=0.196.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.05)=0.050.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba7a8267-d75d-5d4b-bc96-5af23d284353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443236 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.196$ to $0.050.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.196}{0.050}=3.92.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.92 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.92) \\\\ p & ≈1.9708….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443237 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443237 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443237 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443237 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.27.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443237 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf397494-3423-5d84-aaa6-feb204494ce6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443237 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={3}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.27}{9}=0.03.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d97bcc9d-8c7b-5d3a-9dd6-550b8b7b1d07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443238 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =5(0.6)^{4}+3(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.129\\,6+3\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.648+0.233\\,28 \\\\  & =0.881\\,28 \\\\  & ≈0.881.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =5(0.3)^{4}+3(0.3)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.008\\,1+3\\cdot 0.002\\,43 \\\\  & =0.0405+0.007\\,29 \\\\  & =0.047\\,79 \\\\  & ≈0.048.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.881\\,28$ to $0.047\\,79.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.881\\,28}{0.047\\,79}=18.440\\,67…≈18.441.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈18.440\\,67… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(18.440\\,67…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.204… \\\\ p & ≈3.204….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443239 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd55dc02-570c-5acd-bec7-3aaa43923830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443239 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{2}=0.4(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}=5(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1071 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec94fc3e-e8fc-535e-a62b-74b6f854910e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443240 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{3}-(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.216-1\\cdot 0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.296-0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.166\\,4 \\\\  & ≈1.166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size 